Linux is an open-source, Unix-like operating system that is highly customizable, secure, and widely used for servers, desktops, and embedded systems.
Linux is a free and open source operating system whose source code is publicly available under the GNU General Public License (GPL). 
This means anyone can run, study, modify, and redistribute it, provided that any redistributed version remains under the same license terms.
The openness of Linux has made it the largest open source software project in the world, with contributions from thousands of developers globally.
